# Reusable stats panel and unit-card popover plan

This checklist is the implementation source of truth for the following open
item in `docs/project.todo`:

> Add a SoulSearch "stats view" modal-window popover to the base game's unit
> card window, so users can inspect a unit without opening the full SoulSearch
> window.

The work has two inseparable parts:

1. encapsulate the existing Stats panel as a behavior-complete reusable
   component; and
2. use that component in a new popover opened from a DFHack overlay button on
   the vanilla unit card.

Do not mark the parent `docs/project.todo` item complete until every automated
gate in this document has passed.

Automatic startup and default-keybinding setup are owned by
`docs/zero-touch-startup.todo`. This plan owns independent overlay discovery,
widget configuration, and popover activation. Its overlay must remain usable
when automatic SoulSearch bootstrap was missed or disabled, and it must not
become the mechanism that installs the full-window `Ctrl-F` binding.

Record phase results in the `Execution evidence` table at the end of this
document. Every completed phase gate must have a dated entry that identifies
the commands and automated checks that justify marking it complete.

## Target behavior

- [ ] The main SoulSearch window and the unit-card popover instantiate the same
      reusable Stats component.
- [ ] The shared component owns its views, relative layout, rendered records,
      scrolling behavior, sort interaction, hit-testing, and stats tooltips.
- [ ] The main window preserves its current Stats geometry and interaction
      behavior. The only planned copy change is replacing resident-specific
      empty/fallback Stats wording with the generic term `unit` for both hosts.
- [ ] A visible SoulSearch button appears only in the confirmed vanilla
      fortress-mode unit-card focus context or context set (expected root:
      `dwarfmode/ViewSheets/UNIT`).
- [ ] In a clean packaged session with the DFHack overlay framework enabled and
      no saved state for this widget, DFHack discovers and enables the unit-card
      button before the user manually runs any SoulSearch command. Automatic
      DFHack module loading is expected and is not a manual invocation of
      `soulsearch`. SoulSearch must not
      force-enable a globally disabled overlay framework. Afterward, normal
      saved widget enablement and placement take precedence.
- [ ] Activating the button captures the unit currently displayed by the
      vanilla card and opens a compact modal Stats popover for that unit.
- [ ] The popover is a centered, non-draggable, non-resizable, non-persisted
      singleton. Repeated calls to `stats_popover.open(unit)` must not create
      stacked copies. A valid call for a different unit updates that singleton
      to the newly captured unit and resets its sort and scroll state; a call
      for the same unit retains that state. Mouse and CLI overlay activation are
      required only from unit-card focus and are not the re-entry mechanism
      while the modal owns focus.
- [ ] The popover does not open the results list, filter editor, presets, or any
      other part of the full SoulSearch window.
- [ ] Closing the popover returns the player to the same vanilla unit card.

## Architectural constraints

- [ ] Add functionality through DFHack's overlay framework; do not modify or
      retain references to the vanilla unit card's internal widget tree.
- [ ] Capture the selected `df.unit` before showing the new
      `gui.ZScreenModal`.
      Selection lookup must not depend on the popover already being on top.
- [ ] Snapshot only the selected unit when opening the popover. Validate and
      collect that unit before showing a screen; do not collect or search every
      fortress candidate for a single-unit stats view.
- [ ] Keep `stats_presenter.lua` responsible for presentation records and
      formatted content; do not duplicate attribute evaluation or formatting
      in the popover.
- [ ] Define a narrow `SoulSearchStatsSubject` data contract for Stats hosts:
      `unit`, `unit_id`, `row`, `name`, `profession`, and `filter_criteria`.
      Normal search results must satisfy it structurally, and singleton identity
      comparisons must use the stable `unit_id` snapshot.
- [ ] Use the generic term `unit` in shared Stats empty and fallback copy. Do
      not add host-supplied terminology or display options to the subject or
      public popover API.
- [ ] Keep unit collection and Stats-subject construction separate:
  - [ ] `residents.validate_unit_reference(unit)` returns
        `integer|nil, error|nil` and is the one non-throwing validation rule
        shared by collection and the popover API. It returns a stable,
        non-negative integer unit ID or an error before any other unit field is
        read;
  - [ ] `residents.collect_unit(unit)` returns
        `SoulSearchResidentRow|nil, error|nil`;
  - [ ] it checks context first, returning the existing unavailable-context
        error for an unloaded map or non-fortress mode, then rejects a nil or
        malformed unit without asserting and snapshots exactly one valid unit
        without printing;
  - [ ] stale live-identity comparison remains the responsibility of
        `stats_popover.open(unit)`, after safe reference validation and before
        collection;
  - [ ] `stats_subject.from_row(row, filter_criteria)` returns one
        `SoulSearchStatsSubject`; and
  - [ ] the popover composes those operations with an empty criteria sequence.
- [ ] Keep error reporting at the outer activation boundary. Collection and
      popover APIs return errors without printing; mouse/CLI activation reports
      the final error exactly once. After a screen has been shown, its
      `onResize()` lifecycle path is the sole exception: it may report one
      unsupported-size error immediately before dismissing itself.
- [ ] Update `stats_presenter.lua` APIs and LuaDoc to consume
      `SoulSearchStatsSubject`, not the wider search-result contract.
- [ ] Update Stats-specific `ui_format.lua` APIs and LuaDoc, including
      `format_stats_header(subject)`, to consume `SoulSearchStatsSubject`.
      Leave result-list formatter contracts typed as `SoulSearchResult`.
- [ ] Keep reusable column dimensions and hitbox boundaries in a dependency-
      free `stats_layout.lua`. `ui_format.lua` and `stats_panel.lua` may both
      consume it; neither may import the other.
- [ ] Make `stats_layout.lua` the single source for the Stats component's
      minimum content dimensions, including the title, underline, minimum unit
      header, fixed column header, and at least one usable body row.
      `stats_popover_config.lua` must derive the popover screen minimum by adding
      only outer modal chrome instead of duplicating or augmenting the component
      minimum with another body-row allowance.
- [ ] Keep Stats sort validation, defaults, and next-state transitions in a
      pure `stats_sort.lua` module shared by all Stats hosts. Do not move or
      change result-list sort behavior as part of this extraction.
- [ ] Keep sort persistence outside the reusable component. The component may
      own active sort interaction state, but reports changes through a callback
      so each host decides whether and where to save them.
- [ ] Treat programmatic `set_sort(sort)` as silent. Invoke
      `on_sort_change(sort)` only for a user-initiated sort transition, avoiding
      persistence callbacks during initialization or host refresh.
- [ ] Treat sort values as snapshots rather than shared mutable tables.
      Normalize and copy incoming sorts, return a copy from `get_sort()`, and
      pass a copy to `on_sort_change()` so component instances, hosts, and saved
      settings cannot mutate one another through aliases.
- [ ] Keep main-window settings and popover settings independent. Opening or
      sorting the popover must not silently mutate the default SoulSearch
      window's saved settings.
- [ ] Keep tooltip rendering at screen level so it can escape panel clipping.
      The reusable Stats component should expose its current tooltip text to
      either host screen.
- [ ] Extract the screen-level tooltip renderer into `ui_tooltip.lua` with a
      host-agnostic `get_tooltip_text() -> string` owner contract. Use an empty
      string, not `nil`, when no tooltip is active.
- [ ] Register every new internal module in `module_registry.lua` and in the
      pure-Lua test harness where required.
- [ ] Do not add the top-level overlay registration script to the internal
      `module_registry.lua` sequence. The overlay framework owns discovery and
      instantiation of that script; only its internal runtime dependencies
      belong in the SoulSearch reload registry.
- [ ] Keep the overlay registration script separate from the enableable
      `soulsearch.lua` bootstrap owned by `docs/zero-touch-startup.todo`. Do not
      add `--@enable=true` to the overlay script merely to seed the default
      keybinding or initialize the full SoulSearch runtime.
- [ ] Register every SoulSearch-owned popover screen with `screen_registry.lua`
      so `soulsearch reload` and teardown dismiss it safely.
- [ ] Keep registry teardown ownership separate from main-window placement
      ownership. A registered popover must be excluded from the frame list used
      by `screen_registry.place_frame()` and must never shift or cascade a main
      SoulSearch window.
- [ ] Make popover registration and dismissal idempotent across explicit close,
      `onDismiss`, `onDestroy`, reload, and invalid-unit failure paths.
- [ ] Give the popover a stable, distinct focus path such as
      `soulsearch/stats`.
- [ ] Do not let the overlay widget retain a stale local reference to a
      reloadable popover module. Resolve the popover entry point dynamically on
      activation, and make `soulsearch reload` rescan/reconstruct the overlay
      widget before reuse.
- [ ] Clear the overlay registration script's DFHack script environment before
      production rescan so removed globals and class methods cannot survive a
      reload.

## Phase 0 - Baseline and vanilla integration proof

- [ ] Record the current main-window Stats behavior before refactoring:
  - [x] title, underline, unit name, profession, and selected-filter summary;
  - [x] physical, mental, and trait color categories;
  - [x] omission of stats that do not deviate meaningfully from baseline;
  - [x] fixed `Stat` and `Delta` header with independently scrolling rows;
  - [x] stat-name and delta sort cycles and their current default directions;
  - [x] stat-name, sort-header, and delta tooltips;
  - [x] correct tooltip record after scrolling via `start_line_num`;
  - [x] empty/no-selection behavior.
- [x] Verify `dfhack.gui.getSelectedUnit(true)` returns the unit displayed on
      `dwarfmode/ViewSheets/UNIT` before any SoulSearch screen is shown.
- [x] Run the existing automated baseline before source changes:
  - [x] `tools/Build.ps1`;
  - [x] `tools/Test.ps1`; and
  - [x] `git diff --check`.

### Phase 0 gate

- [x] Selected-unit lookup and current Stats behavior are evidence-backed rather
      than assumed.
- [x] Existing automated gates are green before the extraction begins.

## Phase 1 - Characterize the reusable behavior seam

- [x] Expand characterization coverage for all behavior moving out of
      `ui.lua`, `ui_components.lua`, and `ui_layout.lua`:
  - [x] view composition and stable child IDs;
  - [x] dynamic header/columns/body layout;
  - [x] label and delta column boundaries;
  - [x] the three-state sort cycle for both columns;
  - [x] sort defaults and reverse-direction rules;
  - [x] display-record retention, including section gaps;
  - [x] header, delta, and attribute tooltip selection;
  - [x] scrolled-row tooltip indexing;
  - [x] scroll reset/retention on subject and sort changes;
  - [x] current keyboard focus order and which inputs reach the Stats area;
  - [x] the Stats column headers' child-view input ownership: `SortableHeader`
        handles its click locally, while `SoulSearchWindow:onInput()` delegates
        to its superclass before only unhandled global fallback handling;
  - [x] current layout refresh timing during main-window resize; and
  - [x] current persistence behavior: user sort clicks update settings while
        initialization and ordinary Stats refresh do not.
- [x] Characterize the existing one-unit data path with
      `residents.collect_units({unit})[1]` so the later
      `residents.collect_unit(unit)` adapter preserves the same row semantics
      without scanning a candidate scope.
- [x] Add tests for units with:
  - [x] physical, mental, and personality data;
  - [x] no current soul;
  - [x] no meaningful deviations; and
  - [x] missing or fallback display names.
- [x] Define and document the `SoulSearchStatsSubject` contract, including an
      stable `unit_id` snapshot, an empty `filter_criteria` sequence for
      unit-card subjects, and structural compatibility with normal SoulSearch
      results.
- [x] Add characterization tests for the current resident-specific empty and
      fallback strings, then deliberately update the shared expectations from
      `No resident selected.` and `Unknown resident` to `No unit selected.` and
      `Unknown unit`. Record this as an intentional cross-host copy correction,
      not an extraction regression.
- [x] Retain `SoulSearchResidentRow` as the historical internal row-type name
      and explicitly document that its structure supports any compatible
      `df.unit`. Do not introduce a cross-module row-type rename as part of this
      extraction.
- [x] Enumerate the current Stats sort default, normalization, and transition
      cases in characterization tests without moving their implementation yet.
      Keep result-list sort cases separate.

### Phase 1 gate

- [x] Tests fail if the extraction changes existing sort, hitbox, tooltip,
      scrolling, formatting, or empty-state behavior, except for the explicitly
      documented generic-unit copy correction.
- [x] A one-unit row and structurally valid Stats subject can be constructed in
      tests without running a full search, while production ownership remains
      unchanged until Phase 2.

## Phase 2 - Extract the reusable Stats component

- [x] Add a focused internal module, preferably `stats_panel.lua`, containing a
      `widgets.Panel`-derived reusable component.
- [x] Add `stats_subject.lua` with a pure `from_row()` constructor and the
      canonical `SoulSearchStatsSubject` LuaDoc contract.
- [x] Update Stats-specific formatter parameter names and LuaDoc in
      `ui_format.lua`, especially `format_stats_header(subject)`, to match that
      contract without widening or renaming result-list formatter contracts.
- [x] Add `residents.collect_unit(unit)` as a one-row collection adapter that
      returns `row|nil, error|nil` and does not know about Stats subjects or
      presentation fields.
  - [x] add `validate_unit_reference(unit) -> integer|nil, error|nil` as the
        shared non-throwing reference validator and use it before any other unit
        field access;
  - [x] call `get_unavailable_reason()` before unit-reference validation so the
        established unloaded-map/non-fortress error takes precedence;
  - [x] reject nil or malformed units without asserting or printing;
  - [x] snapshot exactly one valid unit with the same row semantics as
        `collect_units({unit})[1]`; and
  - [x] leave stale ID-to-object validation to the popover API.
- [x] Add focused collection tests for valid and nil references, missing,
      non-integer, and negative IDs, an ID access that raises, context-error
      precedence, no printing, and one-row parity with
      `collect_units({unit})[1]`.
- [x] Add dependency-free `stats_layout.lua` before moving layout constants:
  - [x] move reusable label width, value-column position, and hitbox boundaries
        into it;
  - [x] update `ui_format.lua` to consume it; and
  - [x] keep main-window placement coordinates in `ui_layout.lua`.
- [x] Add pure `stats_sort.lua` and make the component, Stats portion of window
      configuration, and tests consume its default, normalization, and
      next-state helpers. Leave result-list sort normalization in
      `window_config.lua`.
- [x] Extract `SoulSearchTooltip` into `ui_tooltip.lua` without importing
      `ui.lua` or depending on `SoulSearchWindow`.
- [x] Give the component a small host-facing API:
  - [x] initial `subject` and `sort` attributes;
  - [x] `set_subject(subject)`;
  - [x] `set_sort(sort)`;
  - [x] `reset_view_state(sort)`, which silently applies the supplied normalized
        sort and resets the scrollable body to its first row;
  - [x] `get_sort()`;
  - [x] `get_tooltip_text()`; and
  - [x] `on_sort_change(sort)` callback.
- [x] Add component-contract tests proving `set_sort()` is silent, user sort
      actions invoke `on_sort_change()`, `reset_view_state()` resets sort and
      scroll without invoking the callback, and relayout after a frame change
      does not require `set_subject()`.
- [x] Add sort-ownership tests proving incoming tables, `get_sort()` results,
      callback values, and two simultaneous Stats component instances cannot
      mutate one another through shared references.
- [x] Move Stats-owned view creation into the component:
  - [x] title and underline;
  - [x] unit header;
  - [x] fixed column header; and
  - [x] scrollable stats body.
- [x] Convert Stats layout from main-window absolute coordinates to
      component-relative coordinates.
- [x] Recompute component-relative frames and hitboxes when the component's
      current `frame_body` changes through its layout lifecycle, independently
      of data refresh.
- [x] Supply component-relative frame calculation through the panel's
      `on_layout(frame_body)` callback so dynamic child frames are established
      during `postComputeFrame()` before the normal child layout pass.
- [x] Prove with focused layout-call coverage that each affected child receives
      one normal layout pass after its frame is updated; do not manually relay
      out children or override `updateLayout()` in the normal implementation.
- [x] Use an `updateLayout()` fallback only if focused automated and live
      evidence shows the target DFHack version cannot support `on_layout()`;
      record that evidence and preserve superclass-first, non-recursive layout.
- [x] Export the component's minimum content dimensions from `stats_layout.lua`
      with one usable body row already included, and test that structural
      minimum independently from the popover's outer-chrome calculation.
- [x] Preserve the current text column geometry and click boundaries unless a
      separately recorded live incompatibility requires a deliberate change.
- [x] Move Stats-owned behavior into the component:
  - [x] presentation refresh and `stats_records` retention;
  - [x] sort-column hit-testing and sort cycling;
  - [x] attribute-row and delta-cell hit-testing; and
  - [x] header, attribute, and delta tooltip selection.
- [x] Continue using `stats_presenter.lua` as the sole component-facing facade
      for Stats text and display records, with `ui_format.lua` remaining the
      underlying text formatter.
- [x] Keep screen-sized tooltip positioning and painting outside the component.
- [x] Move component-internal constants and hit-testing out of the main-window
      `ui_layout.lua`; keep only the component's placement frame there and put
      shared formatting geometry in `stats_layout.lua`.
- [x] Register every module introduced in this phase and update
      `tests/run.lua`, loader stubs, focused suites, and loader-contract tests
      in this phase rather than deferring them to Phase 6.
- [x] Assert dependency-safe registry order explicitly:
  - [x] `ui_layout` precedes `ui_format` for the result widths and filter-action
        metadata that remain main-window-owned;
  - [x] `stats_layout` precedes `ui_format`;
  - [x] `ui_format` precedes `stats_presenter`;
  - [x] `ui_format` precedes `ui_tooltip` because tooltip wrapping consumes
        `ui_format.wrap_text()`;
  - [x] `stats_sort` precedes `window_config` and `stats_panel`;
  - [x] `stats_subject` precedes popover consumers;
  - [x] `ui_tooltip` precedes `ui` and the popover screen; and
  - [x] `stats_presenter`, `attribute_descriptions`, and `stats_layout` precede
        `stats_panel`, which precedes `ui`.

### Phase 2 gate

- [x] The component can be instantiated in an arbitrary parent frame without
      referring to `STATS_LEFT`, the main window, result-list subviews, or
      `window_settings`.
- [x] Component tests cover rendering state, sorting, layout, scrolling, and
      tooltips without constructing the full SoulSearch screen.
- [x] `tools/Build.ps1`, `tools/Test.ps1`, and `git diff --check` pass.

## Phase 3 - Migrate the main SoulSearch window

- [x] Remove the compatibility `create_stats_panel()` and `update_stats_panel()`
      helpers from `ui_components.lua` after this phase replaces their active
      full-window callers with `stats_panel.lua`.
- [x] Replace the existing five loose Stats views with one reusable Stats
      component positioned at the current Stats panel location.
- [x] Keep the `ui_refresh.apply()` owner contract unchanged. Implement
      `SoulSearchWindow:refresh_stats(result)` as the adapter that delegates to
      `self.subviews.stats_panel:set_subject(result)`, preserving the existing
      dispatcher and controlled result-selection callback suppression.
- [x] Initialize the component from the current window identity's saved
      `stats_sort` snapshot.
- [x] Persist sort changes through the component callback using the existing
      window-settings path.
- [x] Keep Stats sort-header click handling inside the reusable component. The
      main window supplies the sort-change callback and remains the owner of
      settings persistence; do not restore root-window hit testing or manual
      post-superclass header dispatch.
- [x] Make the main tooltip aggregator ask the Stats component for Stats-owned
      tooltip text while preserving the priority of unrelated control,
      results, filter, and picker tooltips.
- [x] Render the aggregated tooltip through the shared `ui_tooltip.lua`
      screen-level view.
- [x] Remove superseded Stats fields and methods from `SoulSearchWindow`,
      including duplicated sort, hit-test, tooltip, and refresh ownership.
- [x] Preserve the current Stats panel placement, width, height, divider,
      header behavior, and scroll extent at the default window size.
- [x] Preserve live resizing behavior and refresh the component layout from its
      own current `frame_body`.
- [x] Preserve the main window's keyboard focus/traversal order and ensure the
      nested Stats panel does not consume unrelated input.
- [x] Preserve the Phase 0 scroll reset/retention behavior when selection or
      sort changes call `set_subject()` or `set_sort()`.

### Phase 3 gate

- [x] No main-window Stats rendering or interaction path bypasses the reusable
      component.
- [x] Existing tests pass with the full-window callers migrated.
- [x] `tools/Build.ps1`, `tools/Test.ps1`, and `git diff --check` pass for the
      migrated main-window implementation.

## Phase 4 - Implement the single-unit Stats popover

- [ ] Add a dedicated popover module and `gui.ZScreenModal` implementation
      rather than invoking `ui.open()` for the full SoulSearch window.
- [ ] Define the popover view hierarchy explicitly:
  - [ ] `SoulSearchStatsPopoverScreen` is the `gui.ZScreenModal` owner with the
        distinct `focus_path='soulsearch/stats'`;
  - [ ] its primary child is a centered `widgets.Window` with
        `draggable=false`, `resizable=false`, and `frame_style=gui.FRAME_BOLD`
        so the external modal frame carries the required DFHack signature;
  - [ ] the reusable Stats component and explicit close control are children of
        that window; and
  - [ ] the shared tooltip is a direct screen-level sibling so it can escape
        window and component clipping.
- [ ] Implement
      `stats_popover.open(unit) -> screen|nil, error|nil` as a
      validate-before-show boundary:
  - [ ] return `residents.get_unavailable_reason()` first when the map or game
        mode is unsupported, preserving the collection API's established error;
  - [ ] call `residents.validate_unit_reference(unit)` to safely obtain its
        stable integer unit ID before reading any other unit field;
  - [ ] confirm that unit ID still resolves to the same live unit;
  - [ ] collect exactly one row with `residents.collect_unit(unit)` and
        propagate, but do not print, any error;
  - [ ] construct the subject with `stats_subject.from_row(row, {})`;
  - [ ] construct and show the modal only after those steps succeed; and
  - [ ] return `nil, error` without registry or viewscreen changes on failure.
- [ ] Instantiate the reusable Stats component as the popover window's content.
- [ ] Implement the target window policy: centered, non-draggable,
      non-resizable, non-persisted, and clamped within the screen.
- [ ] Give the popover a compact frame that satisfies the component minimum at
      the exact Phase 0 supported tile-grid size.
- [ ] Add dedicated `stats_popover_config.lua` for popover defaults, minimum
      tile-grid dimensions, centering, and screen-bound clamping. Do not reuse
      the main window's private 90x30 normalization contract.
- [ ] Derive the popover minimum in `stats_popover_config.lua` from the
      `stats_layout.lua` component minimum by adding `gui.FRAME_BOLD` insets,
      then applying minimum outer-dimension constraints for non-overlapping
      close-control, signature, and force-pause badge rendering. The component
      minimum already includes its title and a usable body row; do not restate
      it as independent numeric constants or add either item a second time.
- [ ] Record an exact supported minimum width and height in DFHack tile-grid
      units from `dfhack.screen.getWindowSize()`, then validate the popover at
      that size and record pixel resolution and UI scale separately. Below that
      minimum, it must refuse to open with a concise error rather than claim to
      provide a usable clipped layout.
- [ ] Re-run screen-frame normalization and component layout from the screen's
      `onResize(w, h)` path; dismiss with a concise error if a resize makes the
      tile grid smaller than the supported minimum. Report that lifecycle error
      exactly once, then use the normal modal dismissal path. Do not write pause
      state directly; inherited `gui.ZScreenModal` teardown must restore the
      pre-modal pause state.
- [ ] Call the superclass `onResize(w, h)` implementation before applying
      popover-specific frame normalization and child layout.
- [ ] Use the shared component's existing `Stats` heading as the popover's one
      visible content title; leave the containing window's `frame_title` unset
      so it does not add a second title. Add an explicit close control that does
      not overlap the title or its hitbox at the minimum supported size, plus
      normal `Esc`/right-click dismissal.
- [ ] Rely on `gui.ZScreenModal` for modal input and pause behavior so clicks do
      not activate controls on the vanilla card underneath it.
- [ ] From both paused and unpaused unit cards, verify live that
      `gui.ZScreenModal` blocks input and restores the intended prior pause
      state after dismissal.
- [ ] Do not override `SoulSearchStatsPopoverScreen:onInput()`. Put any custom
      input behavior in the window or its child views and rely on inherited
      `gui.ZScreenModal` handling for `Esc`, right click, focus, and input
      blocking.
- [ ] Implement `onGetSelectedUnit()` on the popover screen and return the
      currently displayed subject's unit. A different-unit singleton update
      must change this result together with the visible subject.
- [ ] Add a shared `ui_tooltip.lua` screen-level sibling driven by
      `stats_panel:get_tooltip_text()`.
- [ ] Define popover sort state as independent from the main window:
  - [ ] initialize every new popover as unsorted
        (`{key=nil, reverse=false, phase=0}`);
  - [ ] retain it while the same unit ID remains displayed;
  - [ ] reset it, along with scroll position, on every new popover instance and
        every valid different-unit singleton replacement through the
        component's `reset_view_state()` API;
  - [ ] do not write it to the default SoulSearch window identity.
- [ ] Enforce a unit-aware singleton popover policy after validating and
      constructing the requested subject:
  - [ ] if no popover exists, create and show one;
  - [ ] treat a cleaned-up, dismissed, destroyed, or otherwise no-longer-shown
        singleton reference as absent instead of attempting to raise it;
  - [ ] if the active popover already shows the same unit ID, raise and return
        it without resetting state;
  - [ ] if it shows a different unit, update the existing screen atomically,
        reset sort and scroll state, then raise and return it; and
  - [ ] if new-unit validation fails, leave the existing popover unchanged and
        return `nil, error`.
- [ ] Register and unregister the popover through `screen_registry.lua` for
      teardown, but mark or classify it as excluded from the main-window frame
      list consumed by `place_frame()`.
- [ ] Route explicit close, `onDismiss`, and `onDestroy` through one idempotent
      cleanup path that unregisters the screen exactly once and clears the
      module-level singleton reference only if it still points to that screen.
      A late callback from an older screen must not clear a newer singleton.
- [ ] Add pure-Lua tests for subject construction, stale-unit rejection,
      nil/malformed-reference rejection before live lookup, unavailable-context
      error precedence, unsupported screen size, same-unit and different-unit
      singleton behavior, failed singleton replacement, default/reset sort and
      scroll state, stable `unit_id` identity, modal window composition and
      signed frame, derived minimum-size/chrome calculation, inherited screen
      input ownership, `onGetSelectedUnit()`, callbacks, close behavior,
      one-time resize error reporting, pause restoration,
      close-then-immediate-reopen behavior, late old-screen teardown, exclusion
      from main-window cascade placement, and teardown bookkeeping where the
      harness can model them.
- [ ] Update `tests/run.lua`, module-registry ordering/contracts, and test
      support loaders for all Phase 4 modules before running this phase's gate.
- [ ] Assert the complete Phase 4 dependency order in the reload registry:
  - [ ] `stats_layout` precedes `stats_popover_config`;
  - [ ] `stats_sort` precedes `stats_popover`; and
  - [ ] `stats_popover_config`, `residents`, `stats_subject`, `stats_panel`,
        `ui_tooltip`, and `screen_registry` all precede `stats_popover`.

### Phase 4 gate

- [ ] The popover renders through the same Stats component used by the main
      window and contains no copied Stats formatting, sort, hit-test, scrolling,
      or tooltip logic.
- [ ] The public popover API is `stats_popover.open(unit)`. It requires only the
      captured unit and does not depend on caller-supplied display options or a
      live `SoulSearchWindow`.
- [ ] `tools/Build.ps1`, `tools/Test.ps1`, and `git diff --check` pass for the
      popover implementation.

## Phase 5 - Add the vanilla unit-card overlay button

- [ ] Add a module-loadable overlay registration script with the target-proven
      repository syntax `--@module=true`.
      Module loading may define the widget class and global `OVERLAY_WIDGETS`,
      but must not look up units, print, register screens, or show UI.
- [ ] Keep that registration script outside the internal module registry; load
      and reload it only through overlay discovery/rescan ownership.
- [ ] Keep this discovery-owned overlay script distinct from the public
      enableable bootstrap in `docs/zero-touch-startup.todo`; do not add an
      enable annotation to the overlay script solely for automatic setup.
- [ ] Define a global `OVERLAY_WIDGETS` entry backed by
      `overlay.OverlayWidget`.
- [ ] Verify the module-loadable registration script is discovered from
      `scripts_modinstalled`, registers its `OVERLAY_WIDGETS` entry, and limits
      it to the confirmed unit-card focus path.
- [ ] Choose and document a stable `OVERLAY_WIDGETS` key/widget identity. Set
      its overlay configuration version deliberately, and increment it only
      when intentionally resetting saved placement/configuration is required.
- [ ] Record the stable script path, widget key, and exact registered name shown
      by `overlay list`; treat all three as compatibility-sensitive identity.
- [ ] Restrict `viewscreens` to the confirmed fortress-mode unit-card focus
      path or path set instead of all of `dwarfmode`.
- [ ] Make the overlay enabled by default for first-time SoulSearch users while
      allowing normal disable/reposition control through DFHack.
- [ ] Treat the overlay framework's global enabled state as a prerequisite, not
      SoulSearch-owned configuration. Do not call `enable overlay` from module
      loading or activation code.
- [ ] Verify that global overlay enablement is distinct from this widget's
      `default_enabled` state. Record that disabling the framework hides the
      widget and is not a condition SoulSearch overrides.
- [ ] Render a clearly branded, compact SoulSearch Stats button at the recorded
      safe default position.
- [ ] Record a safe default overlay-button position at the supported UI scales.
- [ ] Define the button label, width, and height once as shared constants. Give
      the overlay widget a matching fixed frame, and use those same constants
      for rendering and hit-testing so visible bounds and the click target
      cannot drift apart.
- [ ] Implement one shared activation helper that first validates the current
      focus against the confirmed unit-card focus
      path set, then calls `dfhack.gui.getSelectedUnit(true)` before showing a
      screen, calls `stats_popover.open(unit)`, reports any focus, selection, or
      popover error exactly once, and returns only the screen or `nil`. This
      guard applies equally to mouse activation and independently callable
      `overlay_trigger()` CLI activation or an optional user-created binding.
      It must not repurpose or duplicate SoulSearch's default `Ctrl-F` binding,
      which opens `gui/soulsearch`.
- [ ] Define activation ownership explicitly:
  - [ ] render the button through static, non-input child content and perform an
        explicit widget-frame hit-test for `_MOUSE_L` in the overlay widget;
  - [ ] call the shared helper only when that hit-test is inside the rendered
        button rectangle;
  - [ ] mouse `onInput()` returns `true` only after consuming that button click
        and delegates all other input to its superclass;
  - [ ] `overlay_trigger()` calls the same helper and returns the shown screen
        for DFHack CLI or optional user-created-keybinding ownership; and
  - [ ] the resulting popover registers exactly once through its screen
        lifecycle regardless of activation path. `screen_registry.lua` does not
        distinguish mouse from CLI activation and does not become an input
        owner.
- [ ] Treat the modal's `soulsearch/stats` focus as outside the unit-card
      activation guard. Same-unit and different-unit re-entry while the popover
      is active belongs to direct `stats_popover.open(unit)` calls and tests,
      not to a second overlay activation path.
- [ ] Resolve the popover module with `reqscript()` at activation time, or use
      an equivalently reload-safe lookup, so `soulsearch reload` cannot leave
      the widget calling a stale module table.
- [ ] Ensure the overlay widget itself is never attached as a popover subview.
- [ ] Keep the button static and omit `overlay_onupdate()` unless live evidence
      proves it is necessary. Never perform per-tick unit scanning or Stats
      recomputation.
- [ ] Add registration, focus-path, default-state, activation, missing-unit,
      wrong-focus-with-selected-unit, fixed-frame geometry, and exact button-
      hitbox tests with overlay/GUI stubs:
  - [ ] prove the declared frame, rendered label bounds, and hitbox use the same
        button constants;
  - [ ] exercise every button edge and immediately outside each edge; and
  - [ ] prove right click, scrolling, and unrelated clicks pass through without
        opening a popover or consuming vanilla unit-card input.
- [ ] Update `tests/run.lua` and overlay/GUI test support in Phase 5 before
      running this phase's gate.
- [ ] Document the development reload procedure:
      `:lua require('plugins.overlay').rescan()` after script changes.
- [ ] Document that removing or renaming overlay-script globals during
      development additionally requires
      `devel/clear-script-env <overlay-script>` before the rescan.
- [ ] Define the production `soulsearch reload` behavior explicitly:
  - [ ] preserve the automatic bootstrap enabled/disabled state and the
        first-run keybinding decision defined by
        `docs/zero-touch-startup.todo`;
  - [ ] leave exactly one SoulSearch-owned lifecycle callback if the startup
        implementation requires one, without retaining a stale internal module
        table;
  - [ ] do not clear or execute the active top-level `soulsearch.lua`
        environment as an entry in its own internal registry sequence;
  - [ ] dismiss all registered main and popover screens;
  - [ ] capture the old registry's complete script-name list before clearing
        any environment;
  - [ ] clear every module environment in that old list except the registry so
        removed module entries cannot survive;
  - [ ] clear and run `internal/soulsearch/module_registry`, reacquire and
        validate the fresh registry table, and replace the command's cached
        coordinator reference;
  - [ ] clear every module named by the fresh registry's `MODULES` sequence,
        including newly added entries but not the newly loaded registry itself,
        then run that sequence in dependency order;
  - [ ] run `devel/clear-script-env` for the stable overlay registration script
        path without manually executing that script;
  - [ ] always call `require('plugins.overlay').rescan()` so overlay-class
        changes are refreshed as well as dynamic popover dependencies;
  - [ ] leave all SoulSearch UI closed after reload; `gui/soulsearch` remains
        the separate command for opening a fresh main window; and
  - [ ] prove that, after invoking `gui/soulsearch` when needed and returning to
        a unit card, the next button activation uses the current overlay and
        popover code.
- [ ] Add command-level reload tests that model an added module, a removed
      module, a reordered dependency, and a changed registry contract, proving
      the fresh registry generation rather than the cached pre-reload table
      controls reconstruction.

### Phase 5 gate

- [ ] With the overlay framework enabled, `overlay list` discovers the widget
      and `gui/control-panel` can enable or disable its saved widget state.
- [ ] The button appears on the vanilla unit card and nowhere outside its
      confirmed focus context.
- [ ] Activating it always opens Stats for the unit that was visible at click
      time.
- [ ] `tools/Build.ps1`, `tools/Test.ps1`, and `git diff --check` pass for the
      overlay and reload implementation.

## Phase 6 - Automated integration, packaging, and documentation

- [ ] Audit that each prior implementation phase updated `tests/run.lua`, the
      module registry, dependency-order contracts, and test support loaders at
      the time its new suites and modules were introduced. Fix any omissions
      before packaging.
- [ ] Update README/help text to describe:
  - [ ] the unit-card Stats button;
  - [ ] the zero-touch installation behavior and command ownership specified by
        `docs/zero-touch-startup.todo`;
  - [ ] `soulsearch` manual setup/reload behavior versus `gui/soulsearch` UI
        opening behavior;
  - [ ] automatic bootstrap and native SoulSearch enable/disable state versus
        overlay state and default-keybinding ownership;
  - [ ] the distinction between global `enable overlay` state and the widget's
        own enable/disable and reposition state;
  - [ ] the relationship between the compact popover and full SoulSearch.
- [ ] Choose and record the release version before publishing. Update
      `NUMERIC_VERSION` and `DISPLAYED_VERSION` in `src/info.txt` as appropriate,
      and keep that mod-release version separate from the overlay widget's
      configuration-reset `version` policy.
- [ ] Ensure the source and packaged mod contain the overlay registration and
      every runtime dependency.
- [ ] Verify the packaged overlay is discoverable through normal
      `scripts_modinstalled` scanning and does not depend on the `soulsearch`
      command having initialized module state first.
- [ ] Cross-check the packaged cold-start and default-keybinding prerequisites
      against the completed gates in `docs/zero-touch-startup.todo`; do not
      duplicate bootstrap implementation inside the overlay registration.
- [ ] Add command-level coverage proving `soulsearch reload` dismisses all
      registered screens, replaces the cached registry coordinator, reconstructs
      modules from the fresh registry generation, clears the overlay script
      environment, requests an overlay rescan without manually executing the
      overlay script, and leaves UI opening to `gui/soulsearch`.
- [ ] Run the automated closure sequence separately:
  - [ ] `tools/Build.ps1`;
  - [ ] `tools/Test.ps1`;
  - [ ] `tools/Publish.ps1`, which invokes `tools/VerifyPackage.ps1` with its
        required `ZipPath`, `PackageRoot`, and expanded-folder arguments; and
  - [ ] `git diff --check`.
- [ ] Record the nested package-verification result from `tools/Publish.ps1`.
      Do not list or run bare `tools/VerifyPackage.ps1` without its mandatory
      arguments.
- [ ] Record production Lua count, test count, package path, and all command
      results in this document when the phase is executed.

### Phase 6 gate

- [ ] Syntax, pure-Lua behavior, package contents, documentation, and whitespace
      checks are green.
## Definition of done

- [ ] One reusable Stats component owns all shared Stats UI behavior.
- [ ] Both the full SoulSearch window and unit-card popover use it directly.
- [ ] The vanilla unit-card button is discoverable, focused to the correct
      context, configurable, and measured to be inexpensive while idle.
- [ ] Automated build, tests, package verification, and diff checks pass.
- [ ] Documentation and `docs/project.todo` reflect the completed behavior.

